{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794425451,"nonce":"RbAHuuKtpn4Kv3KASbKW5BYT6yQ=","title":"Reported 2026-11-12 from the devops-gitops pilot on Scarab 0.3.2 (EKS cluster, ECR in a different AWS account). Read from the code at v0.3.2; not run on the cluster.","message":"A `build:` step cannot push to ECR with a credential that stays valid. Registry auth is a scoped `REGISTRY_AUTH` dockerconfigjson secret (ADR-0018 amendment) or the forge-derived GHCR / Forgejo credential, copied into a per-Pod Secret by `ensure_registry_secret` (crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:624). An ECR password from `GetAuthorizationToken` expires after 12 hours, so a stored secret goes stale.\n\nTwo places the credential could come from:\n\nA. In the Pod (the pilot's proposal). An init container in the build Pod, running as the step ServiceAccount through IRSA, runs `aws ecr get-login-password` (or an equivalent small binary) and writes config.json into a tmpfs volume that DOCKER_CONFIG points at. Nothing is stored. The authority is the step ServiceAccount's IAM role, which every step Pod in every Project shares: any step can already mint the same token with the AWS SDK, so this adds no new access, and also gives no Project-level control.\n\nB. At launch, in the control plane, the same way the GHCR credential is derived (`BuildConfig::derived_auth`, minted per Attempt, never serialized). An admin-configured, Project- or Environment-scoped \"ECR registry\" entry names the registry and an IAM role the server assumes; the server calls GetAuthorizationToken and mounts the result as the Pod's dockerconfigjson. Scoping and audit stay in Scarab. Needs the server's IAM identity to be allowed to assume the role in the registry's account.\n\nB fits the existing design (scoped credentials, minted at launch, same mount). A is smaller. Either way, `ensure_registry_secret` and the DOCKER_CONFIG mount stay as they are.\n\nRelated, out of this ticket: today every step Pod shares one ServiceAccount, so IRSA permissions given for image pushes reach every Project's steps. Worth a separate look (per-Project ServiceAccounts or role scoping).\n\nWorkaround today: a `privileged` grant (ADR-0039) for a digest-pinned BuildKit image in an Environment; the step runs `aws ecr get-login-password` and buildctl itself. Unverified on EKS.\n\nAcceptance: a `build:` step with `push: true` to an ECR repository in another account succeeds with no stored ECR password, and a Run started 13 hours after setup still pushes.","files":null}]}